    "note": "M1 bars are used only to order a stop and a target that fall in the same higher-timeframe candle. For the M1 timeframe itself no lower resolution exists, so stop-first is always used there."
  },
  "transaction_costs": {
    "model": "net = gross - cost_price * volume, applied once per closed trade",
    "unit": "USD of P&L per unit of simulated volume; cost_price is a price distance in USD (XAUUSD point = 0.01, so 0.46 = 46 points)",
    "sides": "ONE full spread width is charged per closed trade. A market round trip crosses the spread once in total (enter at ask, exit at bid), so charging the full spread once is the complete round trip, not one side.",
    "scales_with_volume": "Yes, linearly: cost = cost_price * volume. Simulated volume is risk_budget / stop_distance (a per-unit size). Because the backtest P&L is volume * price_move, cost also equals (spread_price / stop_distance) * risk_budget in dollars, which matches the real broker cost spread_price * contract_size * lots for any contract size.",
